a new week brings no fresh start no end  to this suffering just another challenge  of survival  kharkiv faced more bombardment overnight  russian forces stepped up heavy shelling  in cities across the country  president zielenski has expressed anger  at what he called silence from western  leaders as russia said its strikes would  continue  we will not forgive the shooting of  unarmed people the destruction of our  infrastructure we will not forgive  hundreds and hundreds of victims  thousands and thousands of sufferings  instead of forgiveness there will be a  day of judgment  tough words and harsh sanctions have  done nothing to deter vladimir putin  in a phone call with his turkish  counterpart the russian president said  the fighting would continue until  moscow's demands are met  and so the inhumanity continues to  efforts to evacuate 200 000 people from  the port city of mariopol have failed  for a second day most people here  sleeping underground after a week of  almost constant shelling  one and a half million people have now  fled the country in just 12 days  brave souls fleeing for their lives like  this 11 year old who traveled by train  to slovakia on his own  arriving with a small bag and a  telephone number written on his hand  his mother has thanked slovakian  authorities  there's a nuclear power plant next to my  town  which the russians are shooting at it  was on fire i can't leave my mother she  can't move independently so i sent my  son alone on a train towards the slovak  border there he met people with a big  heart your small country has people with  big hearts please save our ukrainian  children and give them a safe haven  the world is watching this war unfold in  horror  with a third round of talks between  russia and ukraine expected later  the need for a breakthrough grows ever  urgent  sally lockwood sky news
 

